---
import BlogArticle from '../../components/BlogArticle.astro';
import BlogCode from '../../components/BlogCode.astro';

const contents = [
  { id: 'doubt', label: 'Is it my code or yesterday\'s data?' },
  { id: 'baseline', label: 'Write the baseline down once' },
  { id: 'reset', label: 'Snap back to it' },
  { id: 'uses', label: 'Where a fast reset pays off' },
];
const seedYaml = String.raw`services:
  gcs:
    buckets:
      - name: exports-baseline
        objects:
          - key: fixtures/sample.csv
            content: "id,status\n1,active\n"
  pubsub:
    topics:
      - name: orders-topic
        subscriptions:
          - name: orders-sub`;
const seedCmd = String.raw`localcloud start --local-only
eval "$(localcloud env)"

curl -fsS -X POST "$LOCALCLOUD_GATEWAY/seed" \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/yaml' \
  --data-binary @seed.yaml`;
const resetCmd = String.raw`curl -fsS -X POST "$LOCALCLOUD_GATEWAY/reset?project=local-gcp-project" \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"restore_seed":true}'`;
const resetBody = '{"restore_seed":true}';
const output = `{"message":"Successfully reset state","project":"local-gcp-project","reset":true,
 "seed_restored":true,"status":"success", ...}`;
---

<BlogArticle
  slug="one-second-cloud-state-resets"
  kind="Walkthrough"
  deck="A test fails. Before you can debug your change, you have to rule out the row someone left behind last week. Describe a known-good state in one file and get back to it in under a second, as often as you like."
  primary={{ href: '/docs/seed-data/', label: 'Seed data reference' }}
  secondary={{ href: '/docs/', label: 'Quick start' }}
  {contents}
  outcome="A seed file that defines your baseline buckets and topics, and a one-line reset that restores it while the runtime stays up."
  next={{ posts: ['tdd-google-cloud-watch-mode', 'reproduce-gcp-incidents-locally'], doc: { href: '/docs/seed-data/', label: 'Seed data reference: file formats, projects and supported services' } }}
>
  <section>
    <h2 id="doubt">Is it my code or yesterday's data?</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">Shared environments accumulate. A duplicate key from an aborted run, a message nobody acknowledged, a bucket with last sprint's fixtures in it. Each one is harmless until a test fails and you can't tell whether your change did it.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">The usual answers are a cleanup script that is always slightly out of date, or running the test twice and hoping. Both are ways of working around not knowing what state you started from.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="baseline">Write the baseline down once</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">A seed file describes the resources and data a clean environment should contain. LocalCloud accepts seed data for 18 services, including Cloud Storage, Pub/Sub, BigQuery, Spanner, Cloud SQL, Bigtable, Secret Manager and Firestore.</p>
    <BlogCode label="seed.yaml" code={seedYaml} copy="copy-blog-reset-seed" />
    <BlogCode label="Start and load the baseline" code={seedCmd} copy="copy-blog-reset-load" />
    <p class="mt-4">Check the file into the repository. It replaces the setup code scattered through your test files, and it documents what the tests assume.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="reset">Snap back to it</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">Make whatever mess you need to: delete the bucket, publish a hundred messages, load bad rows. Then reset the project with <code>{resetBody}</code> and LocalCloud clears it and reapplies the last seed you loaded.</p>
    <BlogCode label="Reset to the baseline" code={resetCmd} copy="copy-blog-reset-cmd" />
    <BlogCode label="Output (trimmed)" code={output} />
    <p class="mt-4">The container never restarts, so this takes a few hundred milliseconds. <code>localcloud reset --local-only</code> does the same clear from the CLI.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="uses">Where a fast reset pays off</h2>
    <ul class="site-list mt-5">
      <li><strong>Before a test run.</strong> Call it from a session fixture and every run starts identical. The <a href="/blog/tdd-google-cloud-watch-mode/">watch-mode walkthrough</a> has the fixture.</li>
      <li><strong>While debugging.</strong> When you suspect the data, reset and rerun. If the failure survives, it is your code, and you have just saved yourself the argument.</li>
      <li><strong>Before a demo.</strong> One command returns the environment to the state your script expects.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-4">One thing to know: when Terraform mode is on, seed requests are skipped so Terraform stays the owner of its resources. Keep Terraform tests and seeded tests in separate runtimes.</p>
  </section>
</BlogArticle>
